            <ArticleTitle>Uterine torsion: a missed diagnosis in young girls?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e5-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpag.2007.10.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Uterine torsion is considered exceedingly rare in childhood. Nonspecific symptoms of acute torsion may result in missed diagnosis with long-term complications. We report three girls with uterine torsion and argue that the condition is more common than reported.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Three girls were referred with complex utero-adnexal structural abnormalities diagnosed as an &quot;unusual congenital anomaly.&quot; Further evaluation showed uterine torsion as the underlying event. Corrective surgery was effective in preservation of uterine functions in two patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Uterine torsion may not be rare in young girls. Missed diagnosis of the condition has devastating effects, emphasizing the need for high index of suspicion.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
